# Harden data migration untrusted inputs and localhost privacy

## Scope and existing evidence

Implement concrete legacy-format refusal/migration policy, bounded
adverse-input coverage and the documented localhost credential/privacy fixes.

This is unfinished implementation work. The following original design
records remain accepted theoretical deliverables and are not reopened:

- ATR-P8-001 — archived release design
- ATR-P8-002 — archived release design
- ATR-P8-005 — archived release design

## Read-only legacy notebook.v1 upgrade (partial) — 2026-11-04

The existing native notebook codec accepts its earlier seven-field
`atrament.notebook.v1` profile without `date_text`, interpreting only that
missing optional field as an empty date. Its eight-field canonical form can
be written to a different, explicitly selected destination; opening the old
file alone does not write, replace or rename it.

A new real Rust CLI acceptance test exercises an opted-in preprinted profile
with date placeholder, border, page numbers and paper geometry. Its original
seven-field bytes remain unchanged while the saved eight-field upgrade
matches a modern canonical file exactly. A separate same-path upgrade
refuses without `--replace` and changes source bytes only when the caller
explicitly selects replacement. Both versions generate identical physical
two-page vector PDF and page-two transparent PNG bytes.

The same test refuses an unsupported schema version, unknown field, wrong
type, oversized JSON and invalid UTF-8 before replacing the accepted target.
Run `node --test tests/backend/notebook-profile/composition/lib.mjs` for
the independent real Rust acceptance path. This is a narrow v1 optional-field
compatibility policy, not migration of arbitrary older schema versions.
The localhost credential/privacy and full migration ledger remain open.

## Symlink-free native CLI source admission (partial) — 2026-11-04

The native `atrament-profiles` read-only inputs now reuse the existing Linux
`openat2` selected-file adapter instead of first inspecting a pathname and
then separately calling `File::open`. This covers saved handwriting,
notebook and paper profile inputs, exact glyph TSV files, and selected plain
text or Markdown flow files. It refuses symbolic links at both leaf and
parent components, parent traversal and nonregular files before decoding.
On platforms without the secure open policy the source read refuses rather
than falling back to a racy opener.

A real Rust CLI test generates valid files, reopens each normal source,
exports actual authored-ink PDF content, then refuses linked leaf and parent
paths across eight input flags while keeping an existing selected output
byte-identical. An explicit `..` path also refuses; the original five input
files remain untouched. Run
`node --test tests/backend/profile-editor-cli/composition/main.mjs`.

This is path-resolution hardening, not a filesystem snapshot or a complete
legacy migration. A regular file can still be modified by another process
after opening, and the local runtime and print adapters have independent
admission paths. The wider credential/privacy milestone remains open.

